The routine alg-a computes the antilogarithm of a three-digit mantissa, by pseudo-multiplication, constants in two
parts.

Input: a mantissa m from 0 to 999. Result: 100 times 10 to the power m / 1000, rounded, from 100 to 998: the three
significant digits of the antilogarithm, whose characteristic places the decimal point. A logarithm L = 1000c + m
gives the number s times 10 to the power c - 2.

Call: load m into the accumulator and JMP 09. It returns with the result in the accumulator.
Cells 09-98 (90 cells). It writes cell 99 and, of its own cells, 31, 34, 42, 53, 66-71, 98; nothing else.
Cost: at most 944 instructions a call, 542.0 on average over the domain, counted from the calling JMP to the
return jump. Worst error: 1 in the last digit (units of the third significant digit) against the correctly rounded
value, at m = 1; 247 of the 1,000 inputs are off.
Address bases: cell 54 holds SUB 58; cell 55 holds SUB 61. To move the routine down by d cells, subtract d from
every address in its instructions that points into cells 09-98 and from the address bases; addresses 00 and 99
stay as they are.

